From: Max More <more@chaph.usc.edu>
Date: Mon, 28 Mar 1994 13:24:28 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-3-348] PCR: Grounding; Subjective incorrigibility.

Reilly Jones: 
>Eric again: 
<I make that statement groundlessly; I do not justify it in any way. 
>But the criticism that my statement is groundless or ultimately unprovable 
>will not impress me.

Reilly:
>PCR, if not grounded in subjective purpose, is simply smug nihilism.  Your 
>knowledge becomes entirely dependent on the strength of intellect of  
>others who criticize your positions.  

Reilly, I think you are missing the point of what Eric is saying.  You're 
projecting onto his view things that he doesn't mean, and that are not part 
of PCR (as I understand it).  Although Eric can, as a good PCRist, make a 
statement or hold a  belief groundlessly (so long as he holds it open to 
criticism and revision), that  doesn't mean that PCR is not "grounded in" a 
subjective purpose, in some sense  of "grounded":

The sense in which PCR (and any other belief held by a PCRist) is not 
grounded  is the sense in which a belief is grounded in rational authorities 
-- incorrigible,  undeniable, absolute epistemological processes or 
principles.

There are two senses in which I think we can say PCR is grounded in 
something:   (a) PCR (and all other beliefs of a good PCRist) is grounded in 
its so far having  survived criticism.  This nonjustificational sense of 
"grounding" would be  acceptable to PCR.  (b) PCR *is* grounded in 
subjective purposes in the sense  that, if my one of my goals was not to be 
as rational as possible, I would not be  interested in PCR.  Even if PCR can 
be successfully defended against all  criticisms, it will never appeal to 
persons who care nothing for consistency,  truth, or reason.  In this sense, 
PCR is grounded in one's values and so is not  "smug nihilism".  Note that 
this is not to say that PCR is *justified* in terms of  one's values; it means 
only that one's acceptance of PCR is explained by one's  valuing rationality 
in combination with believing that PCR, so far, provides the  best, most 
consistent, characterization of what it means to be a rationalist.

>PCR relies on the abilities of others to determine right from wrong for you. 
>How is this different from the Skeptics advice to follow convention since you 
>can't be certain of anything?  It isn't any different.  PCR simply leads to 
>conventional authority worship with the ability to know right from wrong 
>entirely dependent on the qualities of those criticizing your beliefs and 
>judgments.  This is nihilism. The smugness sets in because all you have to do,
>is decide who is an authority (who has standing to criticize you) and 
>who isn't. [...]

After all this discussion of PCR, I'm disappointed that you wrote the above.   
Surely you can see that your characterization grossly misrepresents PCR.  
PCR  does not rely on the abilities of others to determine what is right from 
wrong,  though it does suggest that learning, advancing your knowledge, 
and moving  closer to the truth are interpersonal processes.  Generally, 
other people can more  effectively criticize your assumptions because they 
are coming at them from a  different perspective and with different biases.  
This is not to say that you cannot  criticize your own beliefs.  Nor is it to 
say that you cannot apply critical  standards to the critical argument given 
you by others.  You don't accept all  criticisms from others because you can 
weed them out with your own critical  standards, nor do you accept all 
criticisms of supposed (or even actual)  authorities on a topic. I really don't
understand how you can think that PCR  leads to "conventional authority 
worship" when it clearly does the opposite.   PCR requires that you use 
your own judgment in making criticisms, in  evaluating criticisms put to 
you, and in deciding when to reject, modify, or  maintain criticized beliefs 
and presuppositions.

Nor does PCR say you can't be certain (though Bartley never uses "certain" 
and I  don't want to get embroiled in the semantic debate).  It says that you 
can be  *convinced* without being *committed*.  If you like, for 
"convinced" read  "certain".  A PCRist may have beliefs that she is 
convinced of, that she holds to  be practically (but not in principle) beyond 
doubt, that she is certain of.  This is  perfectly rational so long as even 
those beliefs are open to criticism.  One  example (from Bartley):  I do not 
seriously entertain the possibility that the  proposition "I am more than two 
years old" could be false.  I am certain of its  truth. I am not *committed* to
its truth because I am willing to hear criticism of  it (perhaps someone 
could provide convincing evidence that I and the world  were created two 
months ago, with false memories).

 >Eric again: 
<In MATTER AND CONSCIOUSNESS, Paul Churchland discusses the 
>fallibility of our access to our internal mental states. "Subjective" reality
>is observed fallibly and imperfectly, just the same way that objective reality
> is observed. Experimental evidence for this is well established, and 
>Churchland provides some good references to the literature. My observation of
>and reporting on my present "subjective" mental state is neither more nor less
>fallible than my observation of and reporting on the objects on my desk 
>at this moment.

Reilly:
>Churchland's brand of functionalism and his arguments for the fallibility of 
>knowing your own thoughts are rejected by me in favor of the biological 
>epistemology of Edelman as modified by Kauffman's spontaneous order theory 
>applied to the neuronal somatic selection system and Searle's "no deep 
>unconscious" models. It is silly to argue that I don't know what I am thinking
>in subjective reality.  Knower-and-known systems such as this are whole, not 
>fragmented as Churchland posits.  Fallibility within consensual reality is 
>commonplace, that is, when you attempt to communicate your mental states  
>and thoughts to others.

Reilly, I very much want to learn more about Edelman's and Kauffman's 
work.   In fact I welcome and even urge you to write a book review for 
EXTROPY,  and/or a concise but reasonably comprehensive summary or 
this list (or the ExI- Essay List).  From the little I know of their work, I 
don't see how it is  incompatible with Churchland's functionalism 
(psychofunctionalism).  After all,  Churchland's kind of functionalist is tied 
to work in neurobiology; new work in  that field should only lead to 
modifications in psychofunctionalism.

In particular, how does Edelman's and Kauffman's writings speak against 
the  corrigibility of our knowledge of our own mental processes?  The 
incorrigibility  view -- very Cartesian and not what I'd expect to hear from 
you -- seems to be  made problematic not just by theory but by experiment.  
For example,  experiments have demonstrated that first-person reports of 
cognitive processes  of people with eidetic "photographic" memory are in 
error.  The subjects believe  that they are looking at an internal image in 
just the way that you'd look at a  photograph, but cleverly designed 
experiments have shown that they are really  scanning through information 
sequentially (just much faster than the rest of us),  and *not* moving an 
inner eye across an internal image.

WE *can* be mistaken about our own mental events.  We may act on the 
basis of  desires that we claim not to have.  We may mistake the causes of 
our judgments  and choices and incorrectly identify the influences on our 
behavior*.   A dream  pain feels real during the dream, but upon 
awakening we realize that it isn't.   Sensations of extreme cold can be 
mistaken for extreme heat...

* Nisbett and Ross, Human Inference: Strategies and Shortcomings of Social  
Judgment (1980); Gazzaniga and LeDoux, The Integrated Mind  (1978).

If I get it finished this afternoon, I'll post one section of my EXTRO 1 paper
"Pancritical Rationalism:  An Extropic Metacontext for Memetic Progress", 
in the  hope that it will provide a useful one-stop brief reference to what 
PCR actually  says.

I'm happy to see the PCR thread continue so long.  I hope to see all you 
PCR  contestants at EXTRO 1.  

Upward and Outward!

