    Ozone is measured by the Dobson Unit (the guy who first measured
it with a spectrometer). Take a column of ozone above you at 0 deg
celcius and 1 atm. Measure its thickness. .01mm is 1 DU.
>From what I recall, the US ozone layer is about 300 DU, the ozone
in the tropics, about 250 DU. The ozone above Antarctica is about
100 DU I think. The kicker is,  it was measured in the 1950s in the 300s.
   The magnitude difference between the 'hole' and the surrounding 
regions is 1/2. This in itself seems alarming, but the data
in the 50s could have been bad (the spectrometers certainly
weren't as good), or the raw interpretation bad. However, atleast
now we can see ozone variation and increased UV. IMHO, however
shaky the ozone hole theory, it is on less shakier ground
than global warming. Futhermore, humans could survive a surface
temperature rise, melting glaciers, and floods. We'd have more of a 
problem if UV increase started causing reduction in the food supply
(dead animals, lower crop yields etc) That is, until we learn Martian farming.
(in fact, global warming might lead to an increase in farm yield. At least
it would make Canada more farmable)

  Besides that, the economic costs of cutting CFC use are no where near
the economic costs of reducing CO2.

-Ray, who thinks there may be something to the theory, but as always,
I think we should research it more


